WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.080 --> 00:00:05.069
It's great to welcome to the program today,
Alex Vitalii, who's a professor of sociology

00:00:05.069 --> 00:00:10.290
at Brooklyn College and also author of the
book The End of Policing.

00:00:10.290 --> 00:00:12.710
It's so great to have you on, Alex.

00:00:12.710 --> 00:00:13.710
My pleasure.

00:00:13.710 --> 00:00:14.710
Day.

00:00:14.710 --> 00:00:15.710
So this is extremely timely.

00:00:15.710 --> 00:00:18.340
And there's so many different things I want
to ask about.

00:00:18.340 --> 00:00:25.310
You've been studying this issue of different
policing practices and techniques.

00:00:25.310 --> 00:00:30.100
You've looked at militarization of law enforcement,
the expansion of the role of police over the

00:00:30.100 --> 00:00:31.810
last 40 years.

00:00:31.810 --> 00:00:36.000
And right now, one of the big discussions
that's happening in light of the George Floyd

00:00:36.000 --> 00:00:41.430
protests and what is this is not my opinion,
but according to polling, not a particularly

00:00:41.430 --> 00:00:45.970
good handling of policing and race relations
by the current president.

00:00:45.970 --> 00:00:49.650
There are discussions about what should happen
with police.

00:00:49.650 --> 00:00:50.650
Big picture.

00:00:50.650 --> 00:00:54.100
So can you just give us your 30000 foot overview?

00:00:54.100 --> 00:00:58.000
As someone who's been following this for a
long time, the claims that are now being made

00:00:58.000 --> 00:00:59.780
are not new to you.

00:00:59.780 --> 00:01:03.290
What do you think is at the crux of the debate
that's happening now?

00:01:03.290 --> 00:01:09.790
Well, I think that following the police killing
of Mike Brown in Ferguson and Eric Garner

00:01:09.790 --> 00:01:15.440
in New York and so many others, the Obama
administration, a lot of big city mayors said,

00:01:15.440 --> 00:01:20.250
look, we're going to fix policing with a set
of kind of procedural reforms.

00:01:20.250 --> 00:01:25.750
We're going to give them body cameras and
implicit bias training and de-escalation training.

00:01:25.750 --> 00:01:29.860
And things are going to get better and things
have not gotten better.

00:01:29.860 --> 00:01:34.250
You know, the situation out on the streets
remains very problematic.

00:01:34.250 --> 00:01:40.100
And people are fed up with what they feel
are these kinds of simplistic solutions.

00:01:40.100 --> 00:01:47.420
And so what's happening now is a demand to
rethink why we're using the police in all

00:01:47.420 --> 00:01:49.360
the different ways that we're using them.

00:01:49.360 --> 00:01:53.909
Are they really the right people to respond
to mental health emergencies or really are

00:01:53.909 --> 00:01:58.200
they the right people to deal with school
discipline issues and a whole host of issues

00:01:58.200 --> 00:01:59.420
like that?

00:01:59.420 --> 00:02:04.979
So one of the things that sometimes I think
makes these conversations difficult in terms

00:02:04.979 --> 00:02:13.340
of being productive is that the conversation
sometimes exist imagining a different world

00:02:13.340 --> 00:02:15.560
than the one that actually exists.

00:02:15.560 --> 00:02:20.970
So, like, as an example, sometimes we hear
there are countries in Europe where the default

00:02:20.970 --> 00:02:22.610
is not that police carry firearms.

00:02:22.610 --> 00:02:27.090
There is a team that has the firearms and
sometimes the firearms are left in the in

00:02:27.090 --> 00:02:28.090
the police cars.

00:02:28.090 --> 00:02:30.800
But as as a rule, the police aren't walking
around with firearms.

00:02:30.800 --> 00:02:32.010
Wouldn't that be something to look at?

00:02:32.010 --> 00:02:34.849
Now, that sounds very interesting to me.

00:02:34.849 --> 00:02:40.500
One key difference is those countries don't
have the amount of armed civilians that the

00:02:40.500 --> 00:02:41.800
United States has.

00:02:41.800 --> 00:02:46.459
So is that a reasonable example of how we
need to be dealing with things as they are

00:02:46.459 --> 00:02:49.880
as our starting point, rather than things
as we would wish them to be?

00:02:49.880 --> 00:02:56.770
Well, absolutely, I mean, this is an incredibly
practical movement that that is about turning

00:02:56.770 --> 00:03:02.239
people out to city council budget hearings
and lobbying city council members, you know.

00:03:02.239 --> 00:03:08.319
And yes, a lot of it is motivated by a kind
of long range desire or a kind of aspirational

00:03:08.319 --> 00:03:14.800
vision about getting to this better world
where guns are not something that people are

00:03:14.800 --> 00:03:17.340
walking around with as a general case.

00:03:17.340 --> 00:03:21.340
But I think everyone in this movement understands
that's not the world we live in right now

00:03:21.340 --> 00:03:29.160
and that this is about a process of creating
safer communities by dialing back both problems

00:03:29.160 --> 00:03:32.440
in the communities and our overreliance on
policing.

00:03:32.440 --> 00:03:40.890
I want to get into the ideas that are very
at a superficial level called either abolish

00:03:40.890 --> 00:03:43.230
or defund of police.

00:03:43.230 --> 00:03:46.760
Now, I did a piece about this a couple of
weeks ago.

00:03:46.760 --> 00:03:54.780
My thought was there are sources of funding
that should arguably be pared down, funding

00:03:54.780 --> 00:04:01.750
used for militarization, for example, funding
used for certain types of weapons or equipment

00:04:01.750 --> 00:04:05.940
that really don't have a place on streets
but are more fit for a battlefield.

00:04:05.940 --> 00:04:12.050
On the other hand, I'm aware of circumstances
where pulling money may not actually help

00:04:12.050 --> 00:04:17.669
in the sense that we may have a police department
that's already understaffed and understaffed.

00:04:17.669 --> 00:04:22.379
Police department is likely to go after the
lowest hanging fruit as they see it, which

00:04:22.379 --> 00:04:28.360
is let's send our limited police officers
to the places that we currently believe have

00:04:28.360 --> 00:04:29.360
more crime.

00:04:29.360 --> 00:04:32.710
Of course, more police officers in one neighborhood
are going to find more crime in that neighborhood.

00:04:32.710 --> 00:04:36.389
And it sort of perpetuates the problems where
we're actually trying to solve.

00:04:36.389 --> 00:04:44.879
So my instinct is that defund may be not precise
enough in terms of a starting point, but I'm

00:04:44.879 --> 00:04:46.669
curious to hear what you think.

00:04:46.669 --> 00:04:53.620
So, you know, defund was not defund the police
was not really a term that was in wide use

00:04:53.620 --> 00:04:57.219
before the current outbreak of protests.

00:04:57.219 --> 00:05:04.150
What was happening in dozens of cities across
the country were very coherent campaigns about

00:05:04.150 --> 00:05:09.559
creating new public safety initiatives that
people in those communities thought would

00:05:09.559 --> 00:05:14.400
actually make them safer than just relying
on police.

00:05:14.400 --> 00:05:19.949
And what what they would hear from local politicians
was, well, there's no money to do that.

00:05:19.949 --> 00:05:20.949
And they were saying, we have.

00:05:20.949 --> 00:05:25.889
But look, every year you've increased the
police budget by five percent, seven percent.

00:05:25.889 --> 00:05:32.039
They've got all this new money for all these
tricked out tanks and and surveillance equipment.

00:05:32.039 --> 00:05:35.780
And also some of what the police are doing
is actually counterproductive.

00:05:35.780 --> 00:05:39.529
We think it's actually harmful and destabilizing.

00:05:39.529 --> 00:05:47.150
So it's about really identifying specific
evidence driven interventions to address specific

00:05:47.150 --> 00:05:53.599
public safety problems that allow us to dial
back our reliance on policing, which has become

00:05:53.599 --> 00:05:56.330
this tool that's just being used to address
everything.

00:05:56.330 --> 00:06:02.479
What are the best examples of where that has
worked without as as there are some who just

00:06:02.479 --> 00:06:08.960
oppose any reconsideration of how or when
police are deployed under the guise that there

00:06:08.960 --> 00:06:10.129
will be more crime?

00:06:10.129 --> 00:06:11.129
It will be more dangerous.

00:06:11.129 --> 00:06:12.819
The public will be unprotected.

00:06:12.819 --> 00:06:14.860
What what counterpoints do we have?

00:06:14.860 --> 00:06:16.270
Where do these initiatives have worked?

00:06:16.270 --> 00:06:22.259
Sure, well, I think it's become pretty obvious
to people that police should not really be

00:06:22.259 --> 00:06:27.249
in the mental health business, that between
a quarter and a half of all people killed

00:06:27.249 --> 00:06:30.919
by police in the United States are having
a mental health crisis.

00:06:30.919 --> 00:06:36.500
And often the police play a destabilizing
and escalating role in those conflicts and

00:06:36.500 --> 00:06:38.639
those crisis situations.

00:06:38.639 --> 00:06:45.610
And so that's why Albuquerque and Milwaukee
and Los Angeles and a whole host of cities

00:06:45.610 --> 00:06:53.039
are now talking concretely about putting in
place 24 hour crisis intervention capacities

00:06:53.039 --> 00:06:56.740
that would allow them to get the police out
of that business.

00:06:56.740 --> 00:07:01.740
And a lot of them are looking at a model being
used in Oregon called Kahootz that's run by

00:07:01.740 --> 00:07:08.589
a nonprofit clinic that provides exactly this
kind of 24 hour crisis response capacity for

00:07:08.589 --> 00:07:15.460
mental health, calls, homeless people in distress,
someone having a drug overdose or some other

00:07:15.460 --> 00:07:18.669
drug related, you know, low level crisis.

00:07:18.669 --> 00:07:23.539
And the folks in Oregon are extremely happy
with the outcome of this approach.

00:07:23.539 --> 00:07:29.009
And I think we're gonna see a lot more cities
adopt it now as a purely logistical question.

00:07:29.009 --> 00:07:34.840
Very often now it's not only police that show
up, but it's also firefighters with a big

00:07:34.840 --> 00:07:40.369
fire truck and that that can sometimes be
more aggravating to a situation than less

00:07:40.369 --> 00:07:44.189
in these situations where you have mental
health workers that are responding.

00:07:44.189 --> 00:07:46.289
Is it mental health workers and fire?

00:07:46.289 --> 00:07:48.940
Or is it just the mental health workers?

00:07:48.940 --> 00:07:55.399
Well, there might be a need for an E.M.S.
response if the if the situation, you know,

00:07:55.399 --> 00:08:01.779
potentially involves someone who might need
medication or might need to be hospitalized.

00:08:01.779 --> 00:08:06.869
And there is a lot of talk about these co
response models where police would also respond

00:08:06.869 --> 00:08:10.479
with these teams, but the evidence on that
is really thin.

00:08:10.479 --> 00:08:17.210
That that the police presence is not really
adding value to those encounters.

00:08:17.210 --> 00:08:22.650
There may be situations where a mental health
team needs a backup at some point.

00:08:22.650 --> 00:08:28.779
But having the police there from the beginning
of those interactions, much less a fire truck,

00:08:28.779 --> 00:08:30.289
that's generally not helpful, though.

00:08:30.289 --> 00:08:36.400
I would say that for someone having a crisis,
it's precisely the fact that an armed police

00:08:36.400 --> 00:08:43.650
officer who is quite likely to arrest them
or compel them to go to a hospital, that's

00:08:43.650 --> 00:08:47.190
what often makes these encounters dangerous
in the first place.

00:08:47.190 --> 00:08:52.540
And so when we take that police response out
of the mix, the chances of things going really

00:08:52.540 --> 00:09:00.420
south is greatly reduced when it comes to
police training and well, when it comes to

00:09:00.420 --> 00:09:01.420
police training.

00:09:01.420 --> 00:09:03.480
Let's let's open the question there.

00:09:03.480 --> 00:09:10.240
My instinct typically is that workers are
in a position of disadvantage in many relationships

00:09:10.240 --> 00:09:15.820
with employers, and I'm supportive of unionization
and collective bargaining, et cetera.

00:09:15.820 --> 00:09:21.440
There is a case to be made that some elements
that might really improve policing, like,

00:09:21.440 --> 00:09:27.720
for example, a national database of bad actors,
if you concede that most police individually

00:09:27.720 --> 00:09:32.170
are not problematic, but that a lot of the
incidents are happening from a relatively

00:09:32.170 --> 00:09:38.660
small number of officers, some kind of national
bad actor database where police departments

00:09:38.660 --> 00:09:43.870
are required to report to it, but also new
police departments would be required to confer

00:09:43.870 --> 00:09:48.200
with the database to see if the person they're
looking at hiring is on the database.

00:09:48.200 --> 00:09:53.500
That seems to me like it would be very powerful
in eliminating the relatively small percentage

00:09:53.500 --> 00:09:56.930
of bad actors responsible for a lot of the
bad acts.

00:09:56.930 --> 00:09:59.750
That's something I can't imagine unions would
ever go for.

00:09:59.750 --> 00:10:02.840
What's your sense of something like that and
how that should be navigated?

00:10:02.840 --> 00:10:10.880
Well, I do think that a national database
would be useful and and support that and there's

00:10:10.880 --> 00:10:13.770
interestingly some bipartisan support for
it.

00:10:13.770 --> 00:10:21.050
We've seen police unions may not be as quick
to object to that across the board as you

00:10:21.050 --> 00:10:25.840
might imagine, because I think in this moment
of crisis, there's a little bit more openness

00:10:25.840 --> 00:10:32.710
among police leaders and police union leaders
to try to do something about that small percentage

00:10:32.710 --> 00:10:40.120
of officers who generate the most complaints
and who are are fired for clearly just reasons.

00:10:40.120 --> 00:10:44.060
Of course, this is not going to solve all
the problems with policing.

00:10:44.060 --> 00:10:45.060
Right.

00:10:45.060 --> 00:10:51.160
There's a lot of ways in which perfectly lawful,
procedurally proper policing can still cause

00:10:51.160 --> 00:10:56.240
harm because we shouldn't be using police
in that situation in the first place.

00:10:56.240 --> 00:11:00.450
What's the lowest hanging fruit in general
in terms of the things that could be done

00:11:00.450 --> 00:11:03.930
most quickly and easily that would have the
greatest impact?

00:11:03.930 --> 00:11:09.360
Well, in addition to the mental health example
we discussed earlier, I think there's a lot

00:11:09.360 --> 00:11:12.320
of efforts underway to remove police from
schools.

00:11:12.320 --> 00:11:18.400
I think this was a really misguided idea driven
by real misinformation about the nature of

00:11:18.400 --> 00:11:20.450
the problem in Europe.

00:11:20.450 --> 00:11:21.670
They think we're crazy.

00:11:21.670 --> 00:11:23.680
They would never do this.

00:11:23.680 --> 00:11:28.370
We need to get police out of the mental health
abstract, out of the homelessness business,

00:11:28.370 --> 00:11:31.900
responding to the homeless people who are
clearly in distress.

00:11:31.900 --> 00:11:36.450
I think we need a rethink about the war on
drugs.

00:11:36.450 --> 00:11:37.910
That's that's a bigger lift.

00:11:37.910 --> 00:11:42.750
But I think that we've got so much evidence
that shows that turning that problem over

00:11:42.750 --> 00:11:45.790
to police is just such a bad idea.

00:11:45.790 --> 00:11:51.320
What about the issue of police immunity, which
is now being brought up, that there that the

00:11:51.320 --> 00:11:56.850
I guess, generally speaking, the idea that
it's too easy for officers not to be held

00:11:56.850 --> 00:12:02.510
accountable in a particular way that some
in the public would like to see because of

00:12:02.510 --> 00:12:03.510
immunity?

00:12:03.510 --> 00:12:07.500
I mean, that's certainly not low hanging fruit
in the sense that that would be a huge undertaking

00:12:07.500 --> 00:12:08.500
to look at that.

00:12:08.500 --> 00:12:10.550
But how much of a priority do you believe
it should be?

00:12:10.550 --> 00:12:15.940
Well, I don't think it should be a huge priority,
I'm not against it, but I think there's a

00:12:15.940 --> 00:12:20.450
lot of fantasy thinking that this is going
to be some kind of cure all.

00:12:20.450 --> 00:12:26.270
And again, it assumes that all the problems
with policing are about these small number

00:12:26.270 --> 00:12:29.420
of videotaped horrible incidents.

00:12:29.420 --> 00:12:34.870
And the problems of policing are also about
the other 10 million arrests they make every

00:12:34.870 --> 00:12:40.160
year, many of them for things that just shouldn't
be dealt with through policing.

00:12:40.160 --> 00:12:44.820
And so getting rid of Kubi is not going to
affect that in any meaningful way.

00:12:44.820 --> 00:12:49.290
And I also think the political lift, as you
mentioned, is going to be so hard on that

00:12:49.290 --> 00:12:54.650
that it would be better to focus on creating
these alternative community based public safety

00:12:54.650 --> 00:12:59.100
initiatives and trying to to focus on that.

00:12:59.100 --> 00:13:05.900
Instead, when it comes to what some call over
criminalization, the fact that there are activities

00:13:05.900 --> 00:13:07.350
which are criminalized.

00:13:07.350 --> 00:13:10.780
And that's, of course, a societal choice to
make.

00:13:10.780 --> 00:13:14.740
You mentioned the war on drugs, which certainly
would be an example of that generally, but

00:13:14.740 --> 00:13:16.200
outside of the war on drugs.

00:13:16.200 --> 00:13:18.740
Do you think over criminalization is a problem?

00:13:18.740 --> 00:13:21.970
And what's what specific areas would you want
to look at first?

00:13:21.970 --> 00:13:25.810
Yeah, I think it is a major problem.

00:13:25.810 --> 00:13:31.910
The research by the Vera Institute of Justice
showed that there are approximately 10 million

00:13:31.910 --> 00:13:36.710
arrests a year in the United States and the
vast majority of them are for very low level

00:13:36.710 --> 00:13:43.240
fences that are really tied to the broken
windows philosophy of policing, which is about

00:13:43.240 --> 00:13:49.400
criminalizing and micromanaging the behavior
of already very vulnerable communities.

00:13:49.400 --> 00:13:55.720
And it's a way of turning problems of poverty
and joblessness and lack of health care into

00:13:55.720 --> 00:13:57.720
criminal justice problems.

00:13:57.720 --> 00:14:03.340
But that system doesn't have the tools that
are going to help solve people's addiction

00:14:03.340 --> 00:14:07.900
problems, mental health problems, homelessness
problems, joblessness problems.

00:14:07.900 --> 00:14:13.840
And that's really what we've seen over the
last 40 years, is the turning of all these

00:14:13.840 --> 00:14:18.570
social and economic problems into criminal
justice problems.

00:14:18.570 --> 00:14:23.680
And that's why the problem is so much bigger
than just a few officers with an abusive track

00:14:23.680 --> 00:14:24.680
record.

00:14:24.680 --> 00:14:25.680
Isn't that by design?

00:14:25.680 --> 00:14:26.680
In some sense?

00:14:26.680 --> 00:14:31.660
And now I feel like there is certainly a political
split where there is a political contingent

00:14:31.660 --> 00:14:36.500
in the United States that, you know, it's
the same folks who they say they're against

00:14:36.500 --> 00:14:37.500
abortion.

00:14:37.500 --> 00:14:39.310
And you show them a study that says, great.

00:14:39.310 --> 00:14:42.470
We know exactly how to prevent abortion, prevent
unwanted pregnancy.

00:14:42.470 --> 00:14:43.470
Here's how you do it now.

00:14:43.470 --> 00:14:45.120
But we don't we don't like those things.

00:14:45.120 --> 00:14:50.040
And it's about if you have unprotected sex,
you should have to deal with the consequences,

00:14:50.040 --> 00:14:55.320
because that's sort of what's right and moral
in the same ways we've seen.

00:14:55.320 --> 00:15:01.029
It's cheaper to house and give health insurance
to the homeless than it is to absorb the social

00:15:01.029 --> 00:15:03.110
costs of them being on the street.

00:15:03.110 --> 00:15:05.320
Well, no, it doesn't fit into the morality.

00:15:05.320 --> 00:15:10.010
I feel like there's something similar in what
you're describing, and an obstacle to me seems

00:15:10.010 --> 00:15:12.570
the political side of it.

00:15:12.570 --> 00:15:14.320
I think that's exactly right.

00:15:14.320 --> 00:15:17.260
That we've turned a lot of water, really.

00:15:17.260 --> 00:15:24.430
Public health and political and economic problems
into questions of morality and that that fits

00:15:24.430 --> 00:15:31.250
with a kind of broader ideology that says
that, you know, the problems of crime and

00:15:31.250 --> 00:15:36.740
disorder are the result of individual and
group moral failure that will only respond

00:15:36.740 --> 00:15:38.589
to punitive interventions.

00:15:38.589 --> 00:15:44.130
And what this does is it lets politicians
off the hook for actually a dream, addressing

00:15:44.130 --> 00:15:50.480
things like housing market failures and labor
market failures and discrimination in education

00:15:50.480 --> 00:15:52.029
and employment.

00:15:52.029 --> 00:15:56.750
And so they don't have to take responsibility
for that anymore because the problem is those

00:15:56.750 --> 00:15:58.540
people and their bad morality.

00:15:58.540 --> 00:16:04.270
And I think one really powerful example you'll
be interested in is Operation Relentless Pursuit,

00:16:04.270 --> 00:16:09.190
which is the Trump administration's big new
tough on crime initiative, where they're going

00:16:09.190 --> 00:16:15.420
to flood seven U.S. cities with federal agents
and money for local police to get Trump's

00:16:15.420 --> 00:16:16.770
favorite bugaboos.

00:16:16.770 --> 00:16:19.750
You know, the drug cartels and the gang bangers.

00:16:19.750 --> 00:16:20.750
Yeah.

00:16:20.750 --> 00:16:25.120
And what this is about is saying that the
problems of Detroit and Cleveland and Memphis

00:16:25.120 --> 00:16:31.610
and Baltimore are not a failed federal policy
on housing, jobs, health insurance.

00:16:31.610 --> 00:16:33.980
No, it's the gang bangers.

00:16:33.980 --> 00:16:35.570
It's the drug cartels.

00:16:35.570 --> 00:16:39.440
And this is exactly the process that we have
to interrupt.

00:16:39.440 --> 00:16:44.170
We've been speaking with Alex Vitalii, who's
professor of sociology at Brooklyn College.

00:16:44.170 --> 00:16:46.220
The book is The End of Policing.

00:16:46.220 --> 00:16:48.860
Alex, I really appreciate your time and your
insights.

00:16:48.860 --> 00:16:50.189
My pleasure.

